# Line endings are pinned so that a checkout produces the same bytes everywhere.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. This repository was published with `core.autocrlf` set and no
# `.gitattributes`, so git stored LF and checked CRLF out into the working tree
# (`git ls-files --eol` reported `i/lf w/crlf`). That is normally invisible - until
# 0.10.0 added a file manifest that hashes bruhswer's own source. A manifest generated
# on one working copy recorded different bytes from the ones a fresh clone produces, so
# every install would have reported the source as modified.
#
# The hasher in app/security/integrity.py now normalises line endings before hashing, so
# the manifest no longer depends on this. This file fixes the underlying cause rather
# than leaving the next tool that reads bytes off disk to rediscover it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf`: git decides what is text, and every text file is checked out with
# LF on every platform. bruhswer is Windows-only, and nothing it ships needs CRLF -
# Python, PowerShell and the Inno Setup compiler all read LF without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never touched. Listing them is not optional: without it `text=auto` has to
# guess, and a wrong guess on a PNG or an .exe corrupts the file.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary

# The file manifest is generated with LF endings by tools/hash_manifest.py and is
# compared byte for byte, so it must never be translated.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
